2a.With respect to the Union-origin requirements referred to in Articles 11, 12 and 13, the Commission shall adopt delegated acts in accordance with Article 30 to recognise, in whole or in part, content originating in a third country as being of Union origin if all the following conditions are fulfilled:
(a) the third country has provided, in law and in practice, national treatment related to Union products or entities under agreements establishing a free trade area or a customs union or the Agreement on Government Procurement, in relation to all the sectors listed in Annex I;
(b) such inclusion is consistent with the objective to avoid dependencies and with the objective of strengthening the economic security of the Union, including the security of supply chains for the products in question, energy security, and the resilience of the Single Market;
(c) the recognition does not create a significant risk of distortions of competition in the Union market arising from subsidies or other forms of state support granted by the third country in respect of the products concerned;
(d) the third country complies with the commitment to implement the Paris Agreement through its nationally determined contributions;
(e) where the third country has established, or is planning to establish, demand-side measures for low-carbon industrial products or public support schemes comparable to those referred to in Article 12, those measures or schemes provide, in law and in practice, national treatment to Union products or entities;
(f) the inclusion of the third country does not entail a risk of circumvention of the Union origin requirements set out in this Regulation, in particular through the re-export of products or components originating in third countries which are not contained in a delegated act adopted based on this Article, or through the establishment on its territory of undertakings owned and controlled by entities established in third countries that are not within the scope of this Article with the purpose to access to the Union market for such products or components.
Where the above conditions are no longer met, the Commission shall adopt a delegated act in accordance with Article 30 to exclude in whole or in part a third country from any delegated act adopted on this paragraph.
For third countries meeting all of the following criteria, the Commission shall adopt the delegated acts referred to in the first subparagraph no later than three months after the date of entry into force of this Regulation, provided that the conditions set out in points (a) to (f) are fulfilled:
(i) it is a party to the Agreement on Government Procurement;
(ii) it has concluded or signed with the Union an agreement establishing a free trade area or a customs union, where relevant obligations of the Union exist under that agreement;
(iii) it is a full member of the International Energy Agency;
and (iv) it is a party to the Paris Agreement.

2a.With a view to ensuring that the requirements laid down in this Regulation are commensurate with the industrial capacity available in the Union, an ex-ante verification mechanism shall be established to assess the availability of Union industrial capacity before any new mandatory requirement concerning Union origin or low-carbon content becomes applicable. That mechanism shall also regularly monitor the development of Union industrial capacity, with a view to determining whether any adjustments to the applicable requirements are necessary and, where appropriate, enabling such adjustments to be introduced. For each product, material or component concerned, the mechanism shall assess at least:
(a) the current production capacity available within the Union;
(b) the number of suppliers established and operating within the Union;
(c) the price differential compared with equivalent alternatives available on international markets;
(d) the quality of the product and its compliance with applicable technical and certification requirements;
(e) the impact on SMEs, downstream undertakings and exporting undertakings; and
(f) the risk of creating or reinforcing strategic dependencies, whether within or outside the Union.
The information gathered via this mechanism shall be used for the subsequent specification of different minimum percentages of Union content, taking into account both the geographical scope used to determine Union origin and the products, materials or components to which those percentages apply.

