c) more than 60% of its world-wide workforce, including its subsidiaries, was employed in the Union;
d) it owned, directly or through its subsidiaries, all intellectual property rights and know-how developed by it or by its subsidiaries, and had not transferred or exclusively licensed any such rights to the entity controlling it or to any other entity controlled by that entity;
e) it spent, including through its subsidiaries, at least 2 % of its consolidated revenues on research and development activities carried out in the Union, and that amount represented more than half of its total research and development spending;
f) it met the conditions laid down in Article 18(2), points (e) and (f). For that purpose, the economic operator shall be treated as the Union target of a foreign direct investment made by the entity controlling it.
The share referred to in point (c) shall be calculated on the basis of the average number of employees during that financial year. The amounts referred to in point (e) shall be established on the basis of the audited consolidated financial statements for that financial year.
The investment authority shall notify the Commission of the draft certificate, and the Commission may object to it within 30 days. If the Commission objects, the investment authority shall not issue the certificate. Certificates shall be recognised in all Member States and published in a register managed by the Commission. The economic operator shall submit annually to the investment authority a declaration confirming that the conditions laid down in this paragraph continue to be met, accompanied by its audited annual financial statements. Where no change has been notified pursuant to paragraph 1c, the certificate shall be renewed upon request under a simplified procedure. Renewals shall be notified to the Commission, which may object within 30 days. If the Commission objects, the certificate shall not be renewed.
The certificate shall apply only to the economic operator to which it is issued and shall not extend to any other entity controlling, controlled by or under common control with that operator.

2a.The Commission shall determine the method for calculating the award criterion relating to content of Union origin exceeding the applicable minimum threshold, as provided for in paragraph 2c, in particular:
(a) the procedures for calculating the proportion of content of Union EU;
(b) the evidence and documents that may be required from tenderers;
(c) the procedures for considering operations carried out by related undertakings;
(d) the neutralisation of intra-group transfer prices through the application of the arm’s length principle.
Contracting authorities and contracting entities may require any further information necessary to verify the accuracy of the information provided by tenderers.
Where there are objective grounds for doubting the truth or accuracy of this information, they shall carry out or arrange the appropriate checks before awarding the contract.

2b.In addition to the requirements laid down in paragraph 2 of this Article, contracting authorities and contracting entities shall include a factor in the award criteria for the relevant procedures that rewards tenders containing a high proportion of manufacturing operations physically carried out in the Union, within the meaning of Article 3(1), point 41a. That award criterion:
(a) shall be stated in the tender documents prior to the submission of tenders;
(b) shall represent at least 40% of the total weighting of the award criteria;
(c) shall be assessed on the basis of a declaration by the tenderer, accompanied by the necessary supporting evidence and, for the vehicles concerned, the information provided in accordance with Article 14(2b);
(d) shall apply irrespective of the minimum Union origin thresholds and low-carbon emission thresholds laid down in Annexes II and III.
The Commission shall specify the calculation methods, supporting evidence and indicators applicable to each product category covered by Annexes II and III.

4b.Contracting authorities shall design procurement procedures in a manner that facilitates the effective participation of SMEs. Where appropriate, they shall:
(a) divide contracts into lots of a size and scope accessible to SMEs;
(b) provide sufficient time for the preparation and submission of tenders;
(c) impose proportionate documentation and evidence requirements;
(d) apply clear and consistent verification criteria; and adapt technical, financial and administrative requirements so as to avoid the unjustified exclusion of SMEs from public procurement procedures.

2a.When applying the requirements referred to in paragraph 1, Member States shall ensure that:
(a) for steel and cast iron, the requirements laid down in Annex II take into account both Union origin and low-carbon performance;
(b) for cement, concrete and mortar, low-carbon requirements shall apply to cement and concrete, while origin requirements relating to clinker shall take into account whether it originates in countries participating in the EU Emissions Trading System or applying an emissions trading system recognised by the Commission as equivalent;
(c) for chemicals and plastics, the requirements laid down in Annex II shall take into account the use of sustainable carbon sources, including recycled carbon, sustainable biomass and carbon captured and used in products, as applicable;
(d) for motor vehicles, public support schemes shall apply in accordance with the principle of technological neutrality and shall include new zero- and low-emission vehicles, including vehicles running exclusively on eligible fuels, as referred to in Article 3, point (21a).

3b.For the purposes of granting support under this Regulation, projects and equipment shall be assessed on the basis of their total cost of ownership (TCO) over their entire life cycle. That assessment shall take into account at least:
(a) initial capital expenditure (CAPEX);
(b) operating and maintenance expenditure (OPEX);
(c) durability and expected service life;
(d) energy efficiency;
(e) operational availability (uptime);
(f) Public grants received
(g) logistics costs
The Commission shall establish harmonised methodologies for calculating and assessing the total cost of ownership referred to in this paragraph. Those methodologies shall ensure the consistent, transparent and non- discriminatory application of the assessment criteria across the Union.

Article 15a
Measures for the chemical industry
1. The Commission shall develop and implement measures applying the objectives and instruments of this Chapter to the chemical industry, with a view to strengthening its competitiveness and resilience.
Those measures shall be developed in close cooperation with the chemical industry, Member States and other relevant stakeholders and shall take due account of the diversity of chemical products, production processes, value chains and end markets, as well as the technical and economic feasibility of the measures concerned.
2. No later than 12 months after the date of entry into force of this Regulation, the Commission shall adopt a Work Plan setting out the measures to be developed pursuant to this Article. The work plan shall precede the adoption of any implementing acts pursuant to this Article and shall identify, in particular:
(a) the product groups, substances, mixtures, materials or value chains to be addressed;
(b) where appropriate, the relevant downstream or end-use markets;
(c) the type of requirement to be considered, including low-carbon requirements, requirements relating to the use of sustainable carbon sources, Union-origin requirements, or any combination thereof;
(d) the methodology or methodologies required for the implementation and verification of such requirements;
(e) the indicative timetable and sequencing for the preparation and adoption of the measures concerned, taking into account investment cycles, technological readiness, the availability of low-carbon production pathways and the availability of reliable and verifiable data;
(f) the expected impact of the measures on the competitiveness of the Union chemical industry, downstream users, small and medium-sized enterprises, public budgets and security of supply.
3. The work plan shall be developed in close cooperation with the chemical industry. The Commission shall establish a structured consultation process enabling representatives of the relevant chemical sectors and value chains to contribute to the identification, design, sequencing and review of the measures referred to in paragraph 2. The Commission shall take due account of the outcome of that consultation and shall make publicly available a summary of how the views expressed by stakeholders have been reflected in the work plan and in the measures subsequently adopted.
4. On the basis of the Work Plan referred to in paragraph 2, the Commission shall adopt implementing acts establishing Union-level demand-side measures for chemical products. Measures adopted pursuant to this paragraph shall be limited to public procurement and public support schemes and may, where appropriate, amend Annex II in relation to the construction, automotive and other relevant end markets identified in the work plan.
Such implementing acts may, in particular:
(a) add chemical product categories, substances, mixtures or materials to Annex II;
(b) identify chemical input materials used in products or projects covered by Annex II;
(c) establish low-carbon requirements, requirements relating to the use of sustainable carbon sources, Union-origin requirements, or any combination thereof;
(d) specify the conditions and transitional periods applicable to those requirements.
5. Measures adopted pursuant to this Article shall be proportionate, technologically neutral and targeted to clearly identified market failures or strategic dependencies. They may apply to final products or to materials, substances or mixtures incorporated into final products or end-use applications, provided that the corresponding compliance obligations are imposed on the economic operators placing those final products or end-use applications on the market or making them available under the relevant public procurement procedure or public support scheme.
6. Before adopting measures pursuant to paragraph 4, the Commission shall ensure that the methodologies necessary to determine and verify compliance with the applicable low-carbon requirements or requirements relating to the use of sustainable carbon sources are available and sufficiently developed.Such methodologies shall be transparent, verifiable, proportionate and technologically neutral, shall be based, where appropriate, on internationally recognised standards, including relevant ISO standards, and shall take into account the specific characteristics of chemical production and value chains. They shall ensure coherence with relevant Union legislation and avoid unnecessary duplication of reporting or verification requirements.
7. Measures adopted pursuant to this Article shall ensure consistency with other relevant Union legislation, in particular Regulation (EU) 2024/3110, Regulation (EU) 2024/1781 and other Union legislation establishing product-related sustainability or carbon-footprint requirements.
8. The Commission shall review and, where necessary, update the Work Plan at least every three years, in consultation with Member States, the chemical industry and other relevant stakeholders, taking into account technological developments, market conditions, progress in the availability of low-carbon production pathways and sustainable carbon sources, and the impact of measures already adopted pursuant to this Article.
9. The Commission shall also assess the need for additional Union-level market-mobilising instruments, beyond the measures provided for in this Regulation, to support the low-carbon and circular transition of the chemical industry and to strengthen the business case for investments in the Union. That assessment shall examine, in particular, how demand-side instruments operating through downstream and end-use markets can create predictable demand for low-carbon and circular chemical products and thereby support investment along chemical value chains. On the basis of that assessment, the Commission shall, no later than 12 months after the date of entry into force of this Regulation, where appropriate, submit a legislative proposal.

