ANNEX IIIa
Union origin requirements for electrical machinery for construction, infrastructure, lifting, cargo-handling, logistics and industrial operations
Part I – Public procurement procedures
The electrical machinery referred to in Annex I, point 4, intended to be used for building and civil engineering works or for logistical operations that is subject to public procurement procedures falling within the scope of Directive 2014/24/EU or Directive 2014/25/EU and launched on or after [OP: please insert the date = six months after the date of entry into force of this Regulation] shall comply with the following Union origin requirements:
(a) the piece of machinery and its traction battery are assembled within the Union;
(b) the ratio between the total ex-works price of the components of the piece of machinery – excluding its battery – originating in the Union and the total ex-works price of the piece of machinery as a whole – excluding the battery – is at least 70%;
(c) the traction battery of the piece of machinery contains at least three main specific components originating in the Union;
(d) the traction battery of the piece of machinery contains at least five main specific components, among which the battery management system, originating in the Union;
(e) the traction battery of the piece of machinery contains at least five main specific components, among which the battery cells and the battery management system, originating in the Union;
(f) the piece of machinery is designed and developed in the Union, and the entity that owns the design authority and is responsible for the conformity assessment is established in the Union.
The requirements set out in points (b) and (d) shall apply from [OP: please insert date three years after the date of entry into force of this Regulation]. The requirement set out in point (e) shall apply from [OP: please insert date six years after the date of entry into force of this Regulation].
By way of derogation from the requirements set out above, electric tower cranes connected to the grid shall comply with the following Union origin requirements:
1. the piece of machinery is assembled and undergoes final functional and safety tests in the Union;
2. the ratio between the total ex-works price of the components, sub-assemblies and manufacturing operations originating in the Union and the total ex-works price of the piece of machinery as a whole is at least 70%;
3. the piece of machinery is designed and developed in the Union, and the entity that owns the design authority and is responsible for the conformity assessment is established in the Union.
The requirement set out in point 2 shall apply from [OP: please insert date three years after the date of entry into force of this Regulation].
Part II – Public support schemes
For schemes established or updated after [OP: please insert the date = six months after the date of entry into force of this Regulation] that support the purchase, lease, rent or hire purchase of the electrical machinery referred to in Annex I, point 4, Member States, regional or local authorities, bodies governed by public law or associations formed by one or more such authorities or one or more such bodies governed by public law shall ensure that only machinery that complies with the following minimum Union origin requirements are eligible under the scheme:
(a) the piece of machinery and its traction battery are assembled within the Union;
(b) the ratio between the total ex-works price of the components of the piece of machinery – excluding its battery – originating in the Union and the total ex-works price of the piece of machinery as a whole – excluding the battery – is at least 70%;
(c) the traction battery of the piece of machinery contains at least three main specific components originating in the Union;
(d) the traction battery of the piece of machinery contains at least five main specific components, among which the battery management system, originating in the Union;
(e) the traction battery of the piece of machinery contains at least five main specific components, among which the battery cells and the battery management system, originating in the Union;
(f) the piece of machinery is designed and developed in the Union, and the entity that owns the design authority and is responsible for the conformity assessment is established in the Union.
The requirements set out in points (b) and (d) shall apply from [OP: please insert date three years after the date of entry into force of this Regulation]. The requirement set out in point (e) shall apply from [OP: please insert date six years after the date of entry into force of this Regulation].
By way of derogation from the requirements set out above, electric tower cranes connected to the grid shall comply with the following Union origin requirements:
1. the piece of machinery is assembled and undergoes final functional and safety tests in the Union;
2. the ratio between the total ex-works price of the components, sub-assemblies and manufacturing operations originating in the Union and the total ex-works price of the piece of machinery as a whole is at least 70%;
3. the piece of machinery is designed and developed in the Union, and the entity that owns the design authority and is responsible for the conformity assessment is established in the Union.
The requirement set out in point 2 shall apply from [OP: please insert date three years after the date of entry into force of this Regulation].

Part -I
For the purpose of calculating the ratios referred to in this Annex:
(a) components manufactured by the vehicle manufacturer or by an undertaking in its group shall be valued at their full production cost, including materials, direct labour and manufacturing overheads, established on the basis of audited cost accounting; transactions between undertakings in the same group shall be valued at arm’s length conditions;
(b) the origin of those components shall be determined in accordance with Article 7; for castings, the smelting and casting shall have taken place in the Union;
(c) at the request of the manufacturer, the value of Union origin incorporated into components of non-Union origin, including through tier 2 and tier 3 components and castings or forgings, may be taken into account, provided that the value of non-Union origin incorporated into components of Union origin is deducted on the same basis and that this method is applied to all of the vehicle’s components;
(d) profits and selling, marketing and general administration expenses shall not be recorded as Union value;
(e) compliance shall be demonstrated through suppliers’ declarations covering a period of at least 12 months and made available to the competent authorities upon their request.

The requirements set out in points e) and f) apply from [OP: please insert date 3 years after the date of entry into force of this Regulation] for vehicles of categories M1 and N1;
The requirements set out in point c) apply from 2032 for vehicles of categories M1 and N1;
The requirements set out in point d) apply from 2035 for vehicles of categories M1 and N1;
The requirements set out in points c), d), e) and f) apply from 2035 for vehicles of categories N2, N3, M2 and M3.
Twelve months before the date of application of each of the requirements set out in points (c) to (f), the Commission shall assess whether sufficient volumes of the relevant Union-origin components – and, in the case of points (c) and (d), traction batteries incorporating the required combination of those components – are commercially available to vehicle manufacturers.
That assessment shall consider actual production output in the Union, validated automotive-grade capacity, available battery chemistries and formats, processing capacity, binding supply commitments, delivery conditions, and the price differential compared with equivalent non-Union products. Announced or nominal production capacity shall not, by itself, constitute evidence of sufficient availability.
Where the assessment demonstrates that insufficient availability, inadequate technical suitability, or a disproportionate price premium would make compliance excessively difficult, the Commission shall adopt a delegated act postponing the date of application of the relevant requirement or establishing an appropriate temporary derogation.

By way of derogation from points (c) and (d), those requirements shall apply to vehicles of categories N2, N3, M2 and M3 only after the Commission has completed a market assessment of the availability and price level of batteries of Union origin suitable for the heavy-duty vehicle segment, and in any event not before 1 January 2035. That assessment shall address, at least, the following elements:
(a) the battery cell chemistries available;
(b) the maturity of the Union battery ecosystem – including processing capacity, price competitiveness and delivery timelines – and its suitability for the specific needs of heavy-duty vehicles.

